clashes broke out at an eu border  checkpoint on tuesday when polish  security forces used water cannon and  tear gas to push back migrants trying to  cross from belarus the european union  has approved more sanctions against the  belarusian government the eu accuses  belarus of escorting potential asylum  seekers from the middle east to the  polish border  violence breaking out on the belarus  poland border  stones fly one way and water cannon  blast back  seven polish border guards were injured  one of them seriously  migrants trying to break through the  border barricade were pushed back into  belarus  water from the cannon was allegedly  laced with tear gas  in poland border guards and soldiers are  on constant watch  the operation was carried out in several  stages and the migrants had different  tasks there were people behind the front  line throwing stones and other dangerous  objects that gave the front line cover  to attempt to destroy the polish border  checkpoint  polish authorities claim the clash was  controlled and supervised by belarusian  forces  there were many people in the crowd with  face coverings that indicates that they  were belarusian security forces  in the video footage we have they appear  to be people with european facial  features and not migrants  by the evening the situation at the  border crossing had eased  according to belarus state-owned media  leader alexander lukashenko has  authorized a shelter for migrants close  to the border  dw's barbara vezel is at the poland  belarus border and she has more on the  conditions many migrants there are  facing  so they are like punching balls in this  geostrategic game between  the european union poland belarus and  russia  and they simply can't help themselves  there's just a handful of local helpers  operating in this area here they're  finding people in the forest from time  to time who are totally exhausted some  have to be taken to a hospital hospitals  because of hypothermia so their  situation is really dire and there is no  solution no political solution inside  anywhere warsaw isn't budging the  government is sticking to its hard line  nor so far do we see any great movement  on this side of minsk and  alexander lukashenko so nobody here has  really any idea and people say once it  gets really cold really really cold out  here in this forest a real human  catastrophe might happen  for more on the story we can speak to  our brussels bureau chief alexander  fornamen good morning to you alexandra i  want to ask you about uh pushbacks that  is the process of taking people who have  reached eu territory back onto non-eu  territory that is supposed to be illegal  in the eu so what does brussels have to  say about poland's actions on the border  it is illegal because every member state  is  obliged to provide admission in order to  assess individual claims for asylum  however we have to say that poland has  amended its law effectively legalizing  pushbacks and that's what also happened  to some extent in latin and lithuania in  those countries who are affected but by  their border crisis  there has been some criticism from the  european commission the commissioner in  charge said that they have question  marks that pushbacks can never  be  normalized however we have to say that  this issue doesn't seem to be a  priority for the european union for the  european commission that it seems it's  more important to sort of present a  united front against blues because it is  seen also as a geopolitical problem here  so the european union um wants uh to  show that they are not going to give in  to lukashenko and his  attempts to blackmail the european union  a geopolitical problem but are there any  signs that there's progress on diffusing  the situation on the diplomatic level  well i think that there is some movement  there we know that lukashenko was now  ready to speak with european leaders  with the german chancellor in particular  and we also know that the iraqi  government for example they have  announced a first repatriation flight  that is due to take place on thursday so  people who are willing to leave might be  able to do so  and the majority of the people trapped  there are from iraq however we also have  to say that when we look at the scenes  we just saw in the package the standoff  between the belarusian and polish border  guards at the border then  you know security forces using tear gas  and water cannon against migrants this  situation could easily escalate  at the same time you know people are  dying at the border temperatures  continue to drop i mean does the eu in  the meantime have a plan on how to bring  migrants to safety while this political  crisis  continues no i don't think so i don't  think any i don't see anything that  would amount to to a plan here  uh although we have to say that they are  there are politicians from the  german green party for example who are  saying we now need to get those people  out we need to bring them to safety and  to assess their asylum claims however  the majority don't seem to be willing to  do so because as i said they think it's  important to send a strong signal that  no one uh will believe that if they get  to belarus they will be able to get into  the european union and that's of course  a huge problem and and this is just like  a very bad situation for all those who  are now trapped between soldiers and uh  rays of wire  our brussels bureau chief alexander  fundament reporting for us thank you  very much  let's get some more perspective on this  story now we can speak to lotta light  she's the eu director of human rights  watch and she's joining us  from brussels thank you for joining us  here on dw  we have heard proposals within the  european union for example from  lithuania to create a humanitarian  quarter corridor for trapped migrants to  assist them to return to their home  countries  what do you think of that  well i think it is important to assist  those who wish to to return to their  home countries and can do so safely  at the same time there is a  responsibility on the part of eu  countries  to hear asylum claims for those who wish  to apply for asylum  and to do that in an orderly fashion  rather than meeting people with tear gas  water cannons and violence  so both would make sense and are  urgently needed apart from that what is  really  urgent right now  is for poland that has erected sort of a  state of emergency and a restricted zone  to allow immediate access to that zone  on its part of the border  to  to humanitarian organizations  journalists human rights workers and  others  lawyers who can help people with their  claims and so on this is urgent this is  now literally about saving lives well i  want to ask you about poland's  perspective here because poland says it  is protecting its borders what do you  make of that  well poland certainly has a right to  protect its borders but it also has  obligations  towards those who  are trying to enter  poland and apply for asylum  and it is clear  that what we are seeing now is a very  vicious and cynical game of illegal and  brutal pushes on the part of the belarus  authorities and security forces  and pushbacks that are also illegal and  brutal on the part of poland  two wrongs don't make a right and an eu  country should  apply its  international obligations and to treat  people with dignity and humanity is  absolutely urgent now i want to double  down on that because you have written  that belarus is indeed brutally abusing  migrants and asylum seekers in the  situation so  what is the right way for the european  union to take a stand against president  lukashenko but also seek a humane  solution  well the eu is taking a stand on  lukachenko's  ruthless manipulation of migrants  it is introducing additional targeted  sanctions and it is widening the  prospect of such sanctions to also  target those who buy into lukachenko's  very cynical game of using migrants  transporting them effectively  trafficking them to belarus and using  them as as human hostages at the border  to put pressure on the eu so that's one  part of the coin and that pressure seems  to be working now at least better than  words and threats were doing just a few  days ago because there are  some some changes and your correspondent  mentioned that before but at the same  time there is a human crisis at the  border that he that the eu and its  member states are exacerbating  by not treating migrants and asylum  seekers who are being abused  in this game  with the dignity and the rights that  they deserve  we'll have to leave it there lotta like  the eu director of human rights watch  thank you very much for joining us here  on dw  thank you
 

